                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "8161aab6-62d8-32a3-954c-4256ebe1837d",
                        "entry_id": "f3d621e2-d3fb-3c89-a630-6198c741c900",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To lean or incline upward; to cause (something) to lean upward."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "ambitransitive",
                            "literary"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0e9512ff-de41-4727-9a00-d8998baf483f",
                                "sense_id": "8161aab6-62d8-32a3-954c-4256ebe1837d",
                                "text": "1834, Albert Pike, “Sunset” in Prose Sketches and Poems Written in the Western Country, Boston: Light & Horton, pp. 192-193,\nThe western sky is wallen\nWith shadowy mountains, built upon the marge\nOf the horizon, from eve’s purple sheen,\nAnd thin gray clouds, that daringly uplean\nTheir silver cones upon the crimson verge\nOf the high zenith,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cb49bb9b-42d1-453a-81fd-1dc2d09bfa29",
                                "sense_id": "8161aab6-62d8-32a3-954c-4256ebe1837d",
                                "text": "1856, Gold-Pen (pseudonym), “My Cottage” in Poems, Philadelphia: Lippincott, 2nd edition, pp. 188-189,\nI forced the slowly yielding door\nThat ope’d on Sabbath morn no more,\nAnd found all that the winds withstood,\nWas an upleaning piece of wood."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "58afd8d1-29d9-46a8-bdc3-6c5f251fd1f4",
                                "sense_id": "8161aab6-62d8-32a3-954c-4256ebe1837d",
                                "text": "1895, Orelia Key Bell, “And every morning as I passed her bower” in Poems, Philadelphia: Rodgers, p. 181,\n[…] that liquid cadency\nSeep’d thro’ the casement to the birds and me,\nWho upleaning drank, and drinking upleaned more."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "04082b37-53c2-4a91-9a13-1ebd721007ef",
                                "sense_id": "8161aab6-62d8-32a3-954c-4256ebe1837d",
                                "text": "1902, George Macdonald Major, “A Chinatown Idyll” in Lays of Chinatown, New York: The Lloyd Press, 2nd edition, p. 64,\nA rakish hat was tilted o’er his eyes.\nA cigarette, with intermittent fire,\nUpleaned to meet it from his stern set lips."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a515ca66-c71a-42d6-a2a2-f9c223b3f8af",
                                "sense_id": "8161aab6-62d8-32a3-954c-4256ebe1837d",
                                "text": "[…] the snow which covered the glacier was melted into upleaning, icy blades which were extremely difficult to cross […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "be875cb1-93e9-3797-9242-bb02169beeec",
                        "entry_id": "f3d621e2-d3fb-3c89-a630-6198c741c900",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To lean (on something)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive",
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ded26507-1015-4987-89f9-294bddd64a36",
                                "sense_id": "be875cb1-93e9-3797-9242-bb02169beeec",
                                "text": "With that vpleaning on her elbow weake,\nHer alablaster brest she soft did kis,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "033269cb-9d39-4e3f-9953-257a5296eb39",
                                "sense_id": "be875cb1-93e9-3797-9242-bb02169beeec",
                                "text": "[…] thus his carelesse time\nThis shepheard driues, vpleaning on his batt,\nAnd on shrill reedes chaunting his rustick rime,"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
